// Deep-dive layer: Proverbs and Isaiah (selected). Format as in dd_a.js.
export default {
  19: [
    [2, `Wisdom is something you search for like silver`,
      `Proverbs 2 is a single long sentence in Hebrew, built on conditions: “If you receive my words… if you cry out for insight… if you seek it like silver and search for it as for hidden treasures, then you will understand the fear of the LORD” (2:1-5). The language of mining makes wisdom an excavation, not a gift received passively. The result is protection: wisdom will deliver you “from the forbidden woman” and “from the way of evil” (2:12, 16). Proverbs 1-9 is a set of parental lectures to “my son,” set against two women: Woman Wisdom and the foreign (or strange) woman, who stands for folly and seduction. Many scholars read it as an extended allegory about two ways of life, with an ethic about sexual faithfulness at its heart.`,
      [[`Job 28:1-28`, `A poem on mining for silver, where wisdom cannot be found by digging.`], [`Matthew 13:44`, `Treasure hidden in a field.`]],
      [`Wisdom is portrayed as something you work for. What have you excavated slowly in your own life?`, `Job 28 says wisdom cannot be mined. How do you read the two together?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Complete the sentence “If I sought wisdom like silver, I would stop…” with three specific things.`]],
    [3, `Trust, and what it costs to stop leaning on yourself`,
      `Proverbs 3:5-6 are often quoted: “Trust in the LORD with all your heart, and do not lean on your own understanding. In all your ways acknowledge him, and he will make straight your paths.” The chapter lists specific practices: honour the LORD with your wealth and the first fruits of your produce (3:9), accept discipline “for the LORD reproves him whom he loves” (3:12), quoted in Hebrews 12:5-6, and do not withhold good from those to whom it is due, when it is in your power to do it (3:27). Wisdom is described as “a tree of life to those who lay hold of her” (3:18). “The LORD by wisdom founded the earth” (3:19). The same wisdom that made the world is offered to a young man.`,
      [[`Hebrews 12:5-6`, `Discipline as a sign of sonship.`], [`James 2:15-16`, `Doing good in practice, not only in word.`]],
      [`“Do not lean on your own understanding.” Where does your own understanding get in the way?`, `“Do not withhold good from those to whom it is due.” Who is owed something you could give today?`],
      [`rate`, `How easy is it for you to act now on a good you could delay?`, `Hard`, `Easy`]],
    [4, `A father’s inheritance: “Above all else, guard your heart”`,
      `Proverbs 4 is a father’s speech that recalls his own father’s teaching: “Get wisdom; get insight… Prize her highly, and she will exalt you” (4:5, 8). He gives a model of intergenerational teaching. The best-known line is 4:23: “Keep your heart with all vigilance, for from it flow the springs of life.” In Hebrew thought the heart is the seat of thought and will, not only emotion. The path of the wicked is “like deep darkness,” while “the path of the righteous is like the light of dawn, which shines brighter and brighter until full day” (4:18-19). The chapter ends with the body: guard your mouth, eyes and feet (4:24-27).`,
      [[`Matthew 15:18-19`, `Jesus: “out of the heart come evil thoughts.”`], [`Psalm 119:105`, `“Your word is a lamp to my feet.”`]],
      [`The father’s advice is inherited from his father. What have you inherited as advice, and what have you changed?`, `“From it flow the springs of life.” What flows from your heart now?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the “heart” in 4:23?`, `The seat of will and thought, the centre of the person.`, `The emotions, to be guarded against destructive impulses.`]],
    [5, `Drink water from your own cistern`,
      `Proverbs 5 warns against the “forbidden woman” whose lips drip honey but whose end is “bitter as wormwood” (5:3-4). It then offers a positive image: “Drink water from your own cistern, flowing water from your own well. Should your springs be scattered abroad, streams of water in the streets?” (5:15-16). “Let your fountain be blessed, and rejoice in the wife of your youth… let her breasts fill you at all times with delight; be intoxicated always in her love” (5:18-19). The ethic of faithfulness is erotic, not just a restriction. The chapter closes with the observation that “a man’s ways are before the eyes of the LORD” (5:21). The Song of Songs gives a positive picture of the same love.`,
      [[`Song of Songs 4:12-15`, `A garden locked and a fountain sealed.`], [`Hebrews 13:4`, `“Let marriage be held in honour among all.”`]],
      [`The chapter places desire inside faithfulness, not against it. How does that compare with how you were taught to think of desire?`, `“Your springs scattered abroad.” What is the cost of diffused attention?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the best reading of the water imagery?`, `Marital love as a source of life.`, `A general metaphor for faithfulness and focus.`]],
    [6, `Go to the ant, and a list of what God hates`,
      `Proverbs 6 begins with advice on surety (co-signing), laziness (“Go to the ant, O sluggard; consider her ways, and be wise,” 6:6), and a worthless person who winks, scrapes with his foot, and points with his finger (6:12-14). Then comes a numerical saying: “There are six things that the LORD hates, seven that are an abomination to him” (6:16): haughty eyes, a lying tongue, hands that shed innocent blood, a heart that devises wicked plans, feet that make haste to run to evil, a false witness who breathes out lies, and one who sows discord among brothers (6:17-19). The pattern “six… seven” is a typical Hebrew poetic form, also found in Amos 1-2. The seventh item places the destruction of community alongside murder.`,
      [[`Amos 1:3`, `“For three transgressions… and for four.”`], [`James 3:14-16`, `Jealousy and selfish ambition lead to disorder.`]],
      [`Which of the seven do you find most subtle in modern life?`, `Why would “one who sows discord among brothers” be placed at the end of such a list?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the list show about biblical ethics?`, `That speech and community matter as much as violence.`, `That wisdom literature lists vices to be recognised, not rules to be enforced.`]],
    [7, `A lesson told as a story`,
      `Proverbs 7 is a narrative: the father looks through his window and sees “among the simple, a young man lacking sense,” who passes the corner near the house of a woman at twilight (7:6-9). She meets him “dressed as a prostitute, wily of heart,” kisses him, and says her husband has gone on a long journey (7:10-20). “All at once he follows her, as an ox goes to the slaughter” (7:22). The father concludes: “Her house is the way to Sheol, going down to the chambers of death” (7:27). The chapter shows how the proverb teaches by story. Notice also the father’s role as an observer, not someone who intervenes. Wisdom here is the ability to foresee where a path leads.`,
      [[`Genesis 39:7-12`, `Joseph flees from Potiphar’s wife.`], [`2 Samuel 11:2-4`, `David sees Bathsheba from a rooftop.`]],
      [`The young man drifts toward temptation “at twilight.” How much of failure is about timing and location?`, `The story is told by a bystander. What would change if it were told by the young man?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Retell Proverbs 7 as a modern story of drift, without the sexual setting: one person, one path, one step at a time.`]],
    [9, `Two feasts and two women`,
      `Proverbs 9 ends the first section with two invitations. Wisdom “has built her house; she has hewn her seven pillars… She has mixed her wine; she has also set her table. She has sent out her young women to call from the highest places in the town, ‘Whoever is simple, let him turn in here!’” (9:1-4). Folly “is loud; she is seductive and knows nothing” and calls from her seat on the high places with the same words (9:13-16). The two women say almost the same things to the same passers-by, and the difference is the guests: “her guests are in the depths of Sheol” (9:18). The chapter sets “the fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom” (9:10) in its midst.`,
      [[`Luke 14:15-24`, `Jesus’ parable of the great banquet, with servants sent out to invite.`], [`Revelation 17:1-6`, `The harlot’s cup, in the language of Folly.`]],
      [`Both invitations use the same words. What does that say about the difficulty of discernment?`, `What banquet are you at, and who invited you?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the chapter teach about wisdom?`, `Wisdom is a choice between two competing invitations.`, `Wisdom is a gift offered, not a prize earned.`]],
    [11, `Proverbs 10-29 begins: the collection of Solomon`,
      `Proverbs 10:1-22:16 is the first Solomonic collection of sayings, short two-line couplets with no obvious order. Chapter 11 includes: “A false balance is an abomination to the LORD, but a just weight is his delight” (11:1), “When pride comes, then comes disgrace, but with the humble is wisdom” (11:2), “Whoever brings blessing will be enriched, and one who waters will himself be watered” (11:25), and “Whoever troubles his own house will inherit the wind” (11:29). Many proverbs are in antithetical parallel (“but”), showing how Hebrew poetry thinks in contrasts. They are generalisations, not guarantees. Ecclesiastes and Job test these generalisations in specific cases.`,
      [[`Luke 6:38`, `“Give, and it will be given to you.”`], [`Ecclesiastes 9:11`, `“The race is not to the swift.”`]],
      [`A proverb is a general truth, not a promise. How do you read the difference?`, `What is a modern “false balance” in your workplace or community?`],
      [`rate`, `How much do you rely on proverbial wisdom as a guide to life?`, `Little`, `A great deal`]],
    [12, `The tongue and the conscientious person`,
      `Proverbs 12 contains a run of sayings about speech: “Whoever loves discipline loves knowledge, but he who hates reproof is stupid” (12:1). “Anxiety in a man’s heart weighs him down, but a good word makes him glad” (12:25). “There is one whose rash words are like sword thrusts, but the tongue of the wise brings healing” (12:18). “Lying lips are an abomination to the LORD, but those who act faithfully are his delight” (12:22). “A righteous man has regard for the life of his beast, but the mercy of the wicked is cruel” (12:10). The last shows that care for animals is a mark of character. The wise are described as those who listen to advice (12:15).`,
      [[`Ephesians 4:29`, `“Let no corrupting talk come out of your mouths.”`], [`Deuteronomy 25:4`, `The law of the ox, a related ethic.`]],
      [`“A good word makes him glad.” Who needs one from you?`, `A proverb says the wise listen to advice. Whose advice do you actually take?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write one “good word” to someone who is anxious, in a single sentence.`]],
    [13, `Hope deferred`,
      `Proverbs 13 includes: “Hope deferred makes the heart sick, but a desire fulfilled is a tree of life” (13:12). “Whoever walks with the wise becomes wise, but the companion of fools will suffer harm” (13:20). “Whoever spares the rod hates his son, but he who loves him is diligent to discipline him” (13:24), a verse that has been used to justify corporal punishment; scholars note that “rod” (*shevet*) is also a shepherd’s tool for guiding, and the Bible’s own wisdom literature has other voices. “A good man leaves an inheritance to his children’s children” (13:22). The chapter’s first verse, “A wise son hears his father’s instruction, but a scoffer does not listen to rebuke” (13:1), echoes the first section’s setting.`,
      [[`Psalm 23:4`, `“Your rod and your staff, they comfort me.”`], [`Ephesians 6:4`, `“Fathers, do not provoke your children to anger.”`]],
      [`“Hope deferred makes the heart sick.” Where is a long-deferred hope shaping you?`, `How should a modern reader handle 13:24?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read 13:24?`, `A call to consistent guidance, with the “rod” as a shepherd’s tool.`, `A reflection of an ancient culture’s views on discipline that we need not adopt.`]],
    [14, `Proverbs on poverty and honest observation`,
      `Proverbs 14:20 observes bluntly: “The poor is disliked even by his neighbour, but the rich has many friends.” The next verse says, “Whoever despises his neighbour is a sinner, but blessed is he who is generous to the poor” (14:21). The chapter includes “There is a way that seems right to a man, but its end is the way to death” (14:12), a verse repeated in 16:25. “Even in laughter the heart may ache, and the end of joy may be grief” (14:13). “Righteousness exalts a nation, but sin is a reproach to any people” (14:34). “Whoever oppresses a poor man insults his Maker, but he who is generous to the needy honours him” (14:31). Proverbs describes social reality honestly while judging it.`,
      [[`James 2:1-9`, `Against partiality for the rich.`], [`Matthew 25:40`, `What you do to the least, you do to Christ.`]],
      [`The proverb describes how people treat the poor without approving it. What do you do with an honest observation?`, `“There is a way that seems right.” When has something that seemed right turned out otherwise?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the role of observation in Proverbs?`, `To describe the world so readers can navigate it wisely.`, `To prescribe a moral order that readers should adopt.`]],
    [15, `A soft answer`,
      `Proverbs 15 opens with its most-quoted saying: “A soft answer turns away wrath, but a harsh word stirs up anger” (15:1). “The eyes of the LORD are in every place, keeping watch on the evil and the good” (15:3). “A joyful heart makes a cheerful face, but by sorrow of heart the spirit is crushed” (15:13). “Better is a little with the fear of the LORD than great treasure and trouble with it” (15:16). “Better is a dinner of herbs where love is than a fattened ox and hatred with it” (15:17). “Without counsel plans fail, but with many advisers they succeed” (15:22). Many sayings in the chapter concern the effect of speech and the state of the heart, and several proverbs echo one another across the book.`,
      [[`James 3:1-12`, `A long reflection on the power of the tongue.`], [`1 Timothy 6:6-8`, `Godliness with contentment is great gain.`]],
      [`Which would you choose today: the soft answer or the right answer? What does each cost?`, `“Better is a dinner of herbs where love is.” What meal in your life matches that?`],
      [`rate`, `How often do you turn away anger with a soft answer?`, `Rarely`, `Often`]],
    [16, `The plans of the heart, and the answer of the tongue`,
      `Proverbs 16:1-9 forms a cluster about God’s sovereignty and human planning: “The plans of the heart belong to man, but the answer of the tongue is from the LORD” (16:1). “Commit your work to the LORD, and your plans will be established” (16:3). “The heart of man plans his way, but the LORD establishes his steps” (16:9). The chapter also contains a collection of royal sayings (16:10-15), “Pride goes before destruction, and a haughty spirit before a fall” (16:18), and “Whoever is slow to anger is better than the mighty, and he who rules his spirit than he who takes a city” (16:32). The sayings hold together human responsibility and divine direction.`,
      [[`James 4:13-15`, `“If the Lord wills, we will live and do this or that.”`], [`Proverbs 19:21`, `“Many are the plans in the mind of a man, but it is the purpose of the LORD that will stand.”`]],
      [`“The plans of the heart belong to man.” How do you plan while acknowledging you do not control the outcome?`, `“He who rules his spirit than he who takes a city.” What would you do differently if you took that seriously?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a plan for something you want to do this year, and add one line: “But I hold it open to…”`]],
    [17, `A friend loves at all times`,
      `Proverbs 17 includes “A friend loves at all times, and a brother is born for adversity” (17:17). “A joyful heart is good medicine, but a crushed spirit dries up the bones” (17:22). “Whoever covers an offence seeks love, but he who repeats a matter separates close friends” (17:9). “Even a fool who keeps silent is considered wise; when he closes his lips, he is deemed intelligent” (17:28). “Better is a dry morsel with quiet than a house full of feasting with strife” (17:1). “The beginning of strife is like letting out water, so quit before the quarrel breaks out” (17:14). The chapter sees conflict as a flood that is easiest to stop at the start. It also holds justice: “He who justifies the wicked and he who condemns the righteous are both alike an abomination to the LORD” (17:15).`,
      [[`Proverbs 18:24`, `“There is a friend who sticks closer than a brother.”`], [`Matthew 18:15`, `Jesus on addressing a wrong.`]],
      [`“Quit before the quarrel breaks out.” What conflict could you stop at its start?`, `The proverb about a joyful heart as medicine has parallels in modern studies of mood and health. How do you hold wisdom and science together?`],
      [`choose`, `What does “covering an offence” mean?`, `Forgiving and not repeating it.`, `Hiding wrongdoing, which may be unwise.`]],
    [18, `Death and life are in the power of the tongue`,
      `Proverbs 18:21 says, “Death and life are in the power of the tongue, and those who love it will eat its fruits.” The chapter also has “The name of the LORD is a strong tower; the righteous man runs into it and is safe” (18:10), “The one who states his case first seems right, until the other comes and examines him” (18:17), and “A man’s spirit will endure sickness, but a crushed spirit who can bear?” (18:14). “Whoever finds a wife finds a good thing and obtains favour from the LORD” (18:22). “A man who has friends must himself be friendly, but there is a friend who sticks closer than a brother” (18:24). The proverb about hearing both sides (18:17) is among the earliest statements of the right to cross-examination.`,
      [[`James 3:6-10`, `The tongue as a fire and a world of unrighteousness.`], [`Exodus 23:1-3`, `Law on false witness and fairness.`]],
      [`“The one who states his case first seems right.” How do you protect yourself from first impressions?`, `The tongue has the power of life and death. When has a word of yours had that kind of weight?`],
      [`rate`, `How often do you wait to hear the other side before you form a judgement?`, `Rarely`, `Always`]],
    [19, `Lending to the LORD`,
      `Proverbs 19 contains several about the poor: “Better is a poor person who walks in his integrity than one who is crooked in speech and is a fool” (19:1). “Whoever is generous to the poor lends to the LORD, and he will repay him for his deed” (19:17). “Many seek the favour of a generous man, and everyone is a friend to a man who gives gifts” (19:6). “Many are the plans in the mind of a man, but it is the purpose of the LORD that will stand” (19:21). “Discipline your son, for there is hope; do not set your heart on putting him to death” (19:18). Verse 17 treats almsgiving as a loan to God, a line Jesus echoes in Matthew 25:40 and the early church in its collections.`,
      [[`Matthew 25:34-40`, `“As you did it to one of the least of these my brothers.”`], [`2 Corinthians 9:6-8`, `“God loves a cheerful giver.”`]],
      [`“Whoever is generous to the poor lends to the LORD.” Does that change what generosity means to you?`, `The proverb about plans and God’s purpose appears twice in nearby chapters. What does the repetition suggest?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read 19:17?`, `As a promise that giving is never lost.`, `As a view that treats the poor as a place where God can be met.`]],
    [20, `Wine, honesty and a lamp`,
      `Proverbs 20:1 warns, “Wine is a mocker, strong drink a brawler, and whoever is led astray by it is not wise.” It also says, “The spirit of man is the lamp of the LORD, searching all his innermost parts” (20:27). “Who can say, ‘I have made my heart pure; I am clean from my sin’?” (20:9). “Unequal weights and unequal measures are both alike an abomination to the LORD” (20:10). “Do not say, ‘I will repay evil’; wait for the LORD, and he will deliver you” (20:22). “Whoever curses his father or his mother, his lamp will be put out in utter darkness” (20:20). The saying about the spirit as a lamp suggests conscience as a divine instrument for self-knowledge.`,
      [[`Romans 12:17-19`, `Paul on leaving vengeance to God.`], [`1 John 1:8-10`, `The question of who can claim to be without sin.`]],
      [`“The spirit of man is the lamp of the LORD.” What does your conscience say about you today?`, `“Do not say, ‘I will repay evil.’” When have you waited and seen justice come?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the lamp saying?`, `As a description of conscience as God’s gift.`, `As a statement that human insight is limited and needs God’s light.`]],
    [21, `Justice and sacrifice`,
      `Proverbs 21:3 says, “To do righteousness and justice is more acceptable to the LORD than sacrifice.” The prophets say the same (Hosea 6:6; Micah 6:6-8). The chapter says, “Every way of a man is right in his own eyes, but the LORD weighs the heart” (21:2). “Whoever closes his ear to the cry of the poor will himself call out and not be answered” (21:13). “A gift in secret averts anger, and a concealed bribe, strong wrath” (21:14), noted without approval. “The horse is made ready for the day of battle, but the victory belongs to the LORD” (21:31). “Whoever guards his mouth and his tongue keeps himself out of trouble” (21:23). The proverbs gather a mix of ethics, worship and realism.`,
      [[`Hosea 6:6`, `“I desire steadfast love and not sacrifice.”`], [`Matthew 5:23-24`, `Reconciliation before offering a gift.`]],
      [`“Every way of a man is right in his own eyes.” How do you test your own certainty?`, `Why would a book of practical wisdom put justice above sacrifice?`],
      [`rate`, `How often do you check your own motives before acting?`, `Rarely`, `Often`]],
    [22, `Train up a child, and the Thirty Sayings begin`,
      `Proverbs 22:6 reads, “Train up a child in the way he should go; even when he is old he will not depart from it.” It is a general proverb, not a guarantee, and many parents have read it as a promise it does not make. The chapter also says, “The rich and the poor meet together; the LORD is the Maker of them all” (22:2) and “The rich rules over the poor, and the borrower is the slave of the lender” (22:7). At 22:17, a new section begins: “Incline your ear, and hear the words of the wise,” followed by thirty sayings (22:20), which closely parallel the Egyptian *Instruction of Amenemope* (c. 1200 BCE). For example, 22:22-23, “Do not rob the poor, because he is poor,” echoes Amenemope’s “Beware of robbing the poor.”`,
      [[`Ephesians 6:4`, `Parenting that does not provoke.`], [`Proverbs 23:10-11`, `Another saying paralleled in Amenemope.`]],
      [`The sayings of Proverbs share material with Egyptian wisdom. What does that say about how Israel received wisdom?`, `Proverbs 22:6 is a general truth, not a guarantee. How do you hold this with a child who has left the path?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the Amenemope parallel show?`, `That Israel used the common wisdom of the region.`, `That divine inspiration works through the sources and forms of human culture.`]],
    [23, `Wise advice at a rich man’s table`,
      `Proverbs 23 is part of the Thirty Sayings: “When you sit down to eat with a ruler, observe carefully what is before you” (23:1). “Do not toil to acquire wealth; be discerning enough to desist. When your eyes light on it, it is gone, for suddenly it sprouts wings, flying like an eagle toward heaven” (23:4-5). “Do not eat the bread of a man who is stingy” (23:6). “Buy truth, and do not sell it; buy wisdom, instruction, and understanding” (23:23). The chapter ends with a vivid portrait of drunkenness: “Who has woe? Who has sorrow? Who has strife?… Do not look at wine when it is red” (23:29-31), ending with the drinker’s claim, “They struck me, but I did not feel it… I must have another drink” (23:35).`,
      [[`Matthew 6:19-21`, `Do not store up treasures on earth.`], [`Ephesians 5:18`, `“Do not get drunk with wine.”`]],
      [`Wealth “sprouts wings.” What have you chased that flew away?`, `The drunkard’s last words in the chapter are a request for another drink. What does the poem teach about addiction?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a short “proverb” in the style of 23:5 about something you were once sure would last.`]],
    [24, `If you faint in the day of adversity`,
      `Proverbs 24 includes: “If you faint in the day of adversity, your strength is small” (24:10). “If you say, ‘Behold, we did not know this,’ does not he who weighs hearts perceive it?” (24:12). “For the righteous falls seven times and rises again, but the wicked stumble in times of calamity” (24:16). “Do not rejoice when your enemy falls” (24:17). “Do not be envious of evil men” (24:1). The chapter ends with a story: “I passed by the field of a sluggard… and behold, it was all overgrown with thorns… Then I saw and considered it; I looked and received instruction. A little sleep, a little slumber, a little folding of the hands to rest, and poverty will come upon you like a robber” (24:30-34).`,
      [[`Ezekiel 3:16-21`, `The watchman’s responsibility, parallel to 24:11-12.`], [`Romans 12:20`, `Paul quotes the saying about feeding an enemy (Proverbs 25:21-22).`]],
      [`“If you say, ‘We did not know this’.” What do you know and not act on?`, `The writer learns from a sluggard’s field. What do you learn from other people’s neglect?`],
      [`choose`, `What do 24:10-12 ask?`, `Rescue those in danger, since claiming ignorance is no excuse.`, `A general ethic of responsibility for what we can see.`]],
    [25, `The proverbs that Hezekiah’s men copied`,
      `Proverbs 25:1 says, “These also are proverbs of Solomon which the men of Hezekiah king of Judah copied.” The chapter shows editors at work, a rare glimpse of how the collection grew in the eighth century BCE. “It is the glory of God to conceal things, but the glory of kings is to search things out” (25:2). “A word fitly spoken is like apples of gold in a setting of silver” (25:11). “If your enemy is hungry, give him bread to eat, and if he is thirsty, give him water to drink, for you will heap burning coals on his head, and the LORD will reward you” (25:21-22), which Paul quotes in Romans 12:20. “Like cold water to a thirsty soul, so is good news from a far country” (25:25). “A man without self-control is like a city broken into and left without walls” (25:28).`,
      [[`Romans 12:20-21`, `Paul’s use of 25:21-22.`], [`Matthew 5:44`, `“Love your enemies.”`]],
      [`The “burning coals” are debated: shame, repentance, or divine judgement? How do you read it?`, `“The glory of kings is to search things out.” What would that mean for a leader today?`],
      [`choose`, `What are the burning coals?`, `A picture of shame that leads to repentance, an old Egyptian ritual interpretation.`, `Divine retribution deferred, which Paul uses to counsel nonretaliation.`]],
    [27, `Faithful are the wounds of a friend`,
      `Proverbs 27 includes “Do not boast about tomorrow, for you do not know what a day may bring” (27:1). “Faithful are the wounds of a friend; profuse are the kisses of an enemy” (27:6). “Iron sharpens iron, and one man sharpens another” (27:17). “As in water face reflects face, so the heart of man reflects the man” (27:19). “Better is open rebuke than hidden love” (27:5). “Know well the condition of your flocks, and give attention to your herds, for riches do not last forever” (27:23-24). “The crucible is for silver, and the furnace is for gold, and a man is tested by his praise” (27:21). The collection values friendship that includes honest speech and the testing of character by flattery.`,
      [[`James 4:13-16`, `“You do not know what tomorrow will bring.”`], [`Galatians 2:11-14`, `Paul confronts Peter, a wound from a friend.`]],
      [`“Faithful are the wounds of a friend.” Who has told you something hard that you needed?`, `“A man is tested by his praise.” How do you respond when you are praised?`],
      [`rate`, `How open are you to correction from a friend?`, `Closed`, `Open`]],
    [28, `He who conceals his transgressions will not prosper`,
      `Proverbs 28 says, “The wicked flee when no one pursues, but the righteous are bold as a lion” (28:1). “Whoever conceals his transgressions will not prosper, but he who confesses and forsakes them will obtain mercy” (28:13). “Blessed is the one who fears the LORD always, but whoever hardens his heart will fall into calamity” (28:14). “Whoever walks blamelessly will be saved, but he who is crooked in his ways will suddenly fall” (28:18). “A faithful man will abound with blessings, but whoever hastens to be rich will not go unpunished” (28:20). “Whoever turns away his ear from hearing the law, even his prayer is an abomination” (28:9). Verse 13 is a short summary of the doctrine of repentance in the Wisdom tradition.`,
      [[`1 John 1:9`, `“If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive.”`], [`Psalm 32:3-5`, `The silence that wastes away, and the confession that releases.`]],
      [`“He who confesses and forsakes them will obtain mercy.” Why are both parts needed?`, `“The wicked flee when no one pursues.” What do you recognise in that?`],
      [`choose`, `What does 28:9 mean?`, `Prayer without obedience is empty.`, `A rhetorical overstatement to warn against presumption.`]],
    [29, `Where there is no vision`,
      `Proverbs 29:18 in the King James reads, “Where there is no vision, the people perish,” and has been a favourite of leaders. The Hebrew word is *chazon*, which means prophetic revelation, so modern versions have “Where there is no prophetic vision (or revelation) the people cast off restraint, but blessed is he who keeps the law.” The chapter also says, “When the righteous increase, the people rejoice, but when the wicked rule, the people groan” (29:2), and “A king gives stability to the land by justice, but he who exacts gifts ruins it” (29:4). “The righteous know the rights of the poor; the wicked have no such understanding” (29:7). “The fear of man lays a snare, but whoever trusts in the LORD is safe” (29:25).`,
      [[`Amos 8:11-12`, `A famine of hearing the words of the LORD.`], [`1 Samuel 3:1`, `“The word of the LORD was rare in those days; there was no frequent vision.”`]],
      [`How does the shift from “vision” to “revelation” change the verse?`, `“The fear of man lays a snare.” Whose approval limits your freedom?`],
      [`choose`, `What does 29:18 mean?`, `Without a shared sense of God’s direction, a community loses restraint.`, `Without a forward-looking purpose, a people disintegrate (the popular reading).`]],
    [30, `The words of Agur, a thinker among the sages`,
      `Proverbs 30 begins with “the words of Agur son of Jakeh,” an otherwise unknown man who is honest about his limits: “Surely I am too stupid to be a man. I have not the understanding of a man. I have not learned wisdom, nor have I knowledge of the Holy One” (30:2-3). “Who has ascended to heaven and come down?” (30:4). He asks for little: “Give me neither poverty nor riches; feed me with the food that is needful for me, lest I be full and deny you… or lest I be poor and steal” (30:8-9). The chapter has number sayings: “three things are too wonderful for me; four I do not understand: the way of an eagle in the sky, the way of a serpent on a rock, the way of a ship on the high seas, and the way of a man with a virgin” (30:18-19). “Four things on earth are small, but they are exceedingly wise” (30:24): ants, rock badgers, locusts and lizards.`,
      [[`John 3:13`, `“No one has ascended into heaven except he who descended.”`], [`Matthew 6:11`, `“Give us this day our daily bread.”`]],
      [`Agur begins with a confession of ignorance. Why does a wisdom book include this?`, `“Give me neither poverty nor riches.” What would be “the food that is needful” for you?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write your own “neither… nor” prayer in the style of Agur: what you would ask for, and what you would avoid.`]]
  ],
  22: [
    [2, `Swords into plowshares, and a call to walk in the light`,
      `Isaiah 2 opens with a vision that appears also in Micah 4: “In the latter days the mountain of the house of the LORD shall be established as the highest of the mountains… and all the nations shall flow to it… He shall judge between the nations… and they shall beat their swords into plowshares, and their spears into pruning hooks; nation shall not lift up sword against nation, neither shall they learn war anymore” (2:2-4). The line is carved at the United Nations. Then the prophet turns to his own people: “O house of Jacob, come, let us walk in the light of the LORD” (2:5). The rest of the chapter describes “the day of the LORD,” when human pride is brought low and idols are abandoned. The contrast between the vision of peace and the present reality is the key to Isaiah.`,
      [[`Micah 4:1-4`, `The parallel vision of peace.`], [`Joel 3:10`, `The reverse: “Beat your plowshares into swords.”`]],
      [`Isaiah moves from a future vision to “let us walk in the light” now. What does he assume about the link between hope and action?`, `Joel reverses the image. What does this show about how prophets used the same words?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the vision of peace depend on?`, `God’s judgement and instruction from Zion.`, `Human willingness to learn and to walk in God’s ways.`]],
    [3, `A city stripped of its leaders, and a Branch after the ruin`,
      `Isaiah 3 announces that God is removing “support and supply”: soldier, judge, prophet, elder, and “the skilful magician” (3:1-3). Chaos follows, in which “the youth will be insolent to the elder” and a man will seize his brother and say, “You have a cloak; you shall be our leader” (3:4-7), and no one will accept the job. The accusation is economic: “It is you who have devoured the vineyard, the spoil of the poor is in your houses. What do you mean by crushing my people, by grinding the face of the poor?” (3:14-15). The poem then turns on the “daughters of Zion,” who walk haughtily, and lists their finery item by item (3:16-23), a catalogue of luxury. Chapter 4 ends the unit with a promise: “In that day the branch of the LORD shall be beautiful and glorious” (4:2), and a cloud by day and fire by night over Zion (4:5), echoing the exodus.`,
      [[`Isaiah 5:8-10`, `Woe on those who join house to house and field to field.`], [`Amos 4:1-3`, `Another prophet’s address to the wealthy women of Samaria.`]],
      [`The leaders are removed and no one wants the job. What does that say about the relation between leadership and collapse?`, `The prophet lists every item of luxury. What does a catalogue do that a general accusation cannot?`],
      [`choose`, `What does Isaiah 3 suggest about the cause of social breakdown?`, `Injustice by the elite who exploit the poor.`, `A broader loss of every social role, from judge to elder.`]],
    [8, `Immanuel and a son named “Hurry to the Plunder”`,
      `Isaiah 7-8 narrates the Syro-Ephraimite crisis (c. 734 BCE). King Ahaz is afraid of Aram and Israel, and Isaiah meets him with his son Shear-jashub (“a remnant shall return”) and offers a sign. When Ahaz refuses, Isaiah says, “Behold, the virgin (or young woman, *almah*) shall conceive and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel” (7:14), “God with us.” In chapter 8, Isaiah names another son Maher-shalal-hash-baz (“swift is the plunder, speedy is the prey”), and tells the people not to fear what others fear (8:12). Matthew 1:22-23 quotes 7:14 in the Greek, where *almah* is translated *parthenos*. The prophecy had a meaning for Ahaz’s time and was read afresh later.`,
      [[`Matthew 1:22-23`, `Matthew applies Isaiah 7:14 to Jesus’ birth.`], [`Isaiah 9:6-7`, `A royal child promised in the next chapter.`]],
      [`Isaiah’s children carry their names as messages. What name might your life carry?`, `How do you read a prophecy that has both an original setting and a later application?`],
      [`choose`, `Who is Immanuel in Isaiah’s time?`, `A child born in Ahaz’s time as a sign.`, `A figure whose full meaning is found later, in Jesus.`]],
    [10, `Assyria as a rod and a boaster`,
      `Isaiah 10 calls Assyria “the rod of my anger” (10:5), sent against “a godless nation” (Israel), but then turns on the rod: “When the Lord has finished all his work… I will punish the speech of the arrogant heart of the king of Assyria” (10:12). The king boasts, “By the strength of my hand I have done it… I have removed the boundaries of peoples” (10:13). Isaiah answers, “Shall the axe boast over him who hews with it?” (10:15). The oracle gives a theology of history in which an empire is God’s instrument and yet responsible for its own pride. The chapter ends with a vision of the Assyrian army marching on Jerusalem and a promise that “the Lord GOD of hosts will lop the boughs with terrifying power” (10:33).`,
      [[`Habakkuk 1:5-11`, `Babylon as God’s instrument, and the prophet’s protest.`], [`Jeremiah 25:8-14`, `Nebuchadnezzar as “my servant,” and later judged.`]],
      [`An empire is used by God and held responsible for its arrogance. How do you hold both?`, `“Shall the axe boast over him who hews with it?” Who is an axe in your world?`],
      [`choose`, `What does Isaiah 10 teach about history?`, `God can use even hostile powers, without approving them.`, `Empires act for their own reasons, and the prophets reinterpret events.`]],
    [14, `The fall of the king of Babylon, and a morning star`,
      `Isaiah 13-14 contain oracles against Babylon. The taunt song of 14:4-21 mocks a tyrant: “How the oppressor has ceased, the insolent fury ceased!” (14:4). Sheol stirs to greet him: “All of them will answer and say to you: ‘You too have become as weak as we!’” (14:10). Then: “How you are fallen from heaven, O Day Star (*helel*), son of Dawn! How you are cut down to the ground, you who laid the nations low! You said in your heart, ‘I will ascend to heaven; above the stars of God I will set my throne on high’” (14:12-13). The Latin *Lucifer* in the Vulgate led the church fathers, including Origen and Jerome, to read it as the fall of Satan, though the immediate target is a human king and the poem draws on an older myth of a star’s ambition.`,
      [[`Ezekiel 28:11-19`, `The king of Tyre, another taunt song with mythic language.`], [`Luke 10:18`, `“I saw Satan fall like lightning from heaven.”`]],
      [`A taunt song about a king uses cosmic imagery. What does that do to the king?`, `Is it faithful to read the text as about Satan? What do you gain and lose?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read Isaiah 14:12?`, `As a mockery of a human king, with mythic language.`, `As a reference to a fallen angelic being, in the church’s later reading.`]],
    [16, `Oracles against the nations, and a grieving prophet`,
      `Isaiah 13-23 is a collection of oracles against nations: Babylon, Assyria, Philistia, Moab, Damascus, Cush, Egypt, Tyre. Chapter 16 continues the oracle on Moab, and the prophet is unusually sympathetic: “Therefore I weep with the weeping of Jazer for the vine of Sibmah… I drench you with my tears” (16:9). “My heart moans for Moab like a lyre” (16:11). Moab was Israel’s enemy and a descendant of Lot, and the text does not hide that. Chapter 19 contains a surprising vision of Egypt and Assyria: “In that day Israel will be the third with Egypt and Assyria, a blessing in the midst of the earth, whom the LORD of hosts has blessed, saying, ‘Blessed be Egypt my people, and Assyria the work of my hands, and Israel my inheritance’” (19:24-25).`,
      [[`Jeremiah 48:31-36`, `A parallel lament over Moab.`], [`Jonah 4:10-11`, `God’s concern for Nineveh.`]],
      [`The prophet weeps for an enemy’s ruin. What does that do to the book’s message about judgement?`, `The vision of a blessed Egypt and Assyria is startling. How does it fit with the rest of Isaiah?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write four lines of lament for a people or country your own community has considered an enemy.`]],
    [19, `Isaiah walks naked for three years`,
      `In chapter 20, God tells Isaiah to remove his sackcloth and sandals and walk “naked and barefoot” for three years as a sign against Egypt and Cush (20:2-4). The date is the year the Assyrian commander Sargon II besieged Ashdod (711 BCE), which Assyrian records confirm. The point is to warn Judah against relying on Egypt: just as Isaiah is stripped, so Egypt’s captives will be led away. “And the inhabitants of this coastland will say in that day, ‘Behold, this is what has happened to those in whom we hoped and to whom we fled for help’” (20:6). It is an example of a prophet’s own body used as a message, a feature also found in Ezekiel and Hosea. Chapter 19, the oracle on Egypt, looks ahead to a time when Egyptians will worship the LORD.`,
      [[`Ezekiel 4:1-8`, `Ezekiel lies on his side for a sign.`], [`Hosea 1:2-3`, `Hosea’s marriage as a message.`]],
      [`The prophet’s body becomes the message. What does that say about the cost of prophecy?`, `Judah is tempted to trust Egypt. What “Egypt” do you rely on?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the three years?`, `A real, extended act of public protest.`, `A shortened or partial nakedness (stripped of outer garments), as some scholars suggest.`]],
    [21, `The watchman’s question`,
      `Isaiah 21 is an oracle with troubling visions: “The Wilderness of the Sea,” Dumah (Edom) and Arabia. The prophet is shaken: “My heart staggers, horror has appalled me; the twilight I longed for has been turned for me into trembling” (21:4). A watchman cries, “Fallen, fallen is Babylon; and all the images of her gods lie shattered on the ground” (21:9), a line that Revelation 14:8 and 18:2 picks up. Then comes the short, haunting oracle on Dumah: “One is calling to me from Seir, ‘Watchman, what of the night? Watchman, what of the night?’ The watchman says: ‘Morning comes, and also the night. If you will inquire, inquire; come back again’” (21:11-12). The ambiguous reply has made it a favourite of commentators.`,
      [[`Revelation 18:1-3`, `“Fallen, fallen is Babylon the great.”`], [`Habakkuk 2:1-3`, `The prophet stands at his watchpost.`]],
      [`“Morning comes, and also the night.” What do you make of an answer that promises both?`, `The watchman’s role is to see and report. Who plays that role in your community?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the watchman’s answer?`, `A promise of change with a warning that it will not be simple.`, `A deliberately cryptic reply to an outsider’s question.`]],
    [23, `Tyre, the merchant city`,
      `Isaiah 23 is an oracle against Tyre: “Wail, O ships of Tarshish, for Tyre is laid waste, without house or harbour!” (23:1). The Phoenician city of Tyre was a trade power with colonies across the Mediterranean, and “whose merchants were princes, whose traders were the honoured of the earth” (23:8). The prophet says the LORD has planned this “to defile the pompous pride of all glory” (23:9). After seventy years, Tyre will return “to her wages… and will prostitute herself with all the kingdoms of the world,” but her profit “will be holy to the LORD” and will provide for “those who dwell before the LORD” (23:15-18). Ezekiel 26-28 give a longer oracle. Alexander besieged Tyre in 332 BCE after a seven-month siege.`,
      [[`Ezekiel 27:1-36`, `A long lament over Tyre as a ship.`], [`Revelation 18:11-19`, `Merchants mourning Babylon’s fall, in language borrowed from Tyre.`]],
      [`The end of the oracle suggests Tyre’s wealth will serve the LORD’s people. What do you make of that?`, `Why would a prophet care about a distant merchant city?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the ending suggest?`, `Even the world’s commerce will be turned to God’s purposes.`, `A later editorial addition that softens the judgement.`]],
    [25, `A feast for all peoples, and death swallowed up`,
      `Isaiah 24-27, sometimes called the “Isaiah Apocalypse,” describes a judgement on the whole earth and a final hope. Chapter 25 says, “On this mountain the LORD of hosts will make for all peoples a feast of rich food, a feast of well-aged wine… And he will swallow up on this mountain the covering that is cast over all peoples… He will swallow up death forever; and the Lord GOD will wipe away tears from all faces” (25:6-8). Paul quotes verse 8 in 1 Corinthians 15:54, and Revelation 7:17 and 21:4 draw on it. Chapter 26:19 adds, “Your dead shall live; their bodies shall rise. You who dwell in the dust, awake and sing for joy!” It is one of the earliest passages in the Hebrew Bible that speaks of resurrection.`,
      [[`1 Corinthians 15:54-55`, `Paul: “Death is swallowed up in victory.”`], [`Revelation 21:3-4`, `God wipes away every tear.`]],
      [`The feast is “for all peoples.” How does that change how you think of the final hope?`, `Isaiah 26:19 speaks of the dead rising. How do you read that against the earlier Hebrew view of Sheol?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the “death swallowed up” promise?`, `A literal end of death in God’s future.`, `A poetic hope of national restoration that later generations read in a fuller sense.`]],
    [27, `A song of a vineyard, reversed`,
      `Isaiah 27 begins with Leviathan: “In that day the LORD with his hard and great and strong sword will punish Leviathan the fleeing serpent… and he will slay the dragon that is in the sea” (27:1). Then a second vineyard song, a reversal of Isaiah 5: “In that day, ‘A pleasant vineyard, sing of it! I, the LORD, am its keeper; every moment I water it… I have no wrath’” (27:2-4). “In days to come Jacob shall take root, Israel shall blossom and put forth shoots, and fill the whole world with fruit” (27:6). Chapter 28 turns back to the failures of Ephraim’s drunkards and Jerusalem’s priests and prophets, who “reel with wine… they stumble in giving judgement” (28:7). A famous line: “Therefore thus says the Lord GOD, ‘Behold, I am the one who has laid as a foundation in Zion, a stone, a tested stone, a precious cornerstone, of a sure foundation: ‘Whoever believes will not be in haste’” (28:16).`,
      [[`Isaiah 5:1-7`, `The first vineyard song.`], [`1 Peter 2:6`, `Peter quotes Isaiah 28:16.`]],
      [`The second vineyard song says “I have no wrath.” How does it answer the first?`, `“Whoever believes will not be in haste.” How does faith relate to patience?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the reversal of the vineyard song suggest?`, `God’s ultimate purpose is restoration, not only judgement.`, `A later hopeful editor’s answer to an earlier text of judgement.`]],
    [29, `Lips that honour, hearts that are far`,
      `Isaiah 29 begins with a siege of “Ariel,” a symbolic name for Jerusalem, and a judgement: “This people draw near with their mouth and honour me with their lips, while their hearts are far from me, and their fear of me is a commandment taught by men” (29:13). Jesus quotes this in Mark 7:6-7 against traditions that nullify God’s word. The prophet says the vision has become like a sealed scroll: “And the vision of all this has become to you like the words of a book that is sealed” (29:11), one to those who can read and one to those who cannot. He also says, “Shall the potter be regarded as the clay?” (29:16), and promises that “the deaf shall hear… and the eyes of the blind shall see” (29:18).`,
      [[`Mark 7:5-8`, `Jesus applies the verse to the tradition of the elders.`], [`Romans 9:20-21`, `Paul’s potter image.`]],
      [`“Their hearts are far from me.” What practices of yours might be “commandments taught by men”?`, `The vision is like a sealed book. What have you not been able to read?`],
      [`rate`, `How easy is it for you to notice when your worship has become routine?`, `Hard`, `Easy`]],
    [30, `In returning and rest you shall be saved`,
      `Isaiah 30 condemns an alliance with Egypt: “Woe to the rebellious children… who carry out a plan, but not mine… who set out to go down to Egypt” (30:1-2). Then comes a line often quoted: “For thus said the Lord GOD, the Holy One of Israel, ‘In returning and rest you shall be saved; in quietness and in trust shall be your strength.’ But you were unwilling” (30:15). The people say, “No! We will flee upon horses” (30:16). Yet God “waits to be gracious to you… Blessed are all those who wait for him” (30:18). “And your ears shall hear a word behind you, saying, ‘This is the way, walk in it,’ when you turn to the right or when you turn to the left” (30:21). The passage links trust and political strategy.`,
      [[`Isaiah 31:1-3`, `“Woe to those who go down to Egypt for help.”`], [`Psalm 20:7`, `“Some trust in chariots and some in horses.”`]],
      [`“In quietness and in trust shall be your strength. But you were unwilling.” What are you unwilling to rest in?`, `“This is the way, walk in it.” When have you heard a voice like this?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write the “horses” you would flee upon in a modern crisis, and what “returning and rest” would look like instead.`]],
    [32, `The righteous king and the complacent women`,
      `Isaiah 32 starts with a promise: “Behold, a king will reign in righteousness, and princes will rule in justice. Each will be like a hiding place from the wind, a shelter from the storm” (32:1-2). Then a warning for “complacent women” (32:9-14) whose comfort will vanish, “until the Spirit is poured upon us from on high, and the wilderness becomes a fruitful field” (32:15). “The effect of righteousness will be peace, and the result of righteousness, quietness and trust forever” (32:17). Chapter 33 laments a betrayer and gives a prayer: “O LORD, be gracious to us; we wait for you” (33:2), and the picture of a righteous person who “walks righteously and speaks uprightly” and “dwells on the heights” (33:15-16).`,
      [[`Acts 2:17`, `The Spirit poured out, echoing 32:15.`], [`Psalm 15:1-5`, `Who may dwell on God’s holy hill.`]],
      [`Isaiah says justice produces peace. How does that compare with political ideas about peace?`, `The “complacent women” are warned of lost comfort. Who today needs that warning, and who delivers it?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the passage say about the Spirit?`, `That God’s Spirit is the source of a renewed society.`, `That the prophet looked ahead to a future outpouring.`]],
    [34, `A day of vengeance and a desert that blooms`,
      `Isaiah 34 is a harsh poem of judgement on the nations, centred on Edom: “For the LORD is enraged against all the nations… He has devoted them to destruction” (34:2). The land turns to pitch and sulphur, with desert animals as the only inhabitants. Then chapter 35 answers with the opposite: “The wilderness and the dry land shall be glad; the desert shall rejoice and blossom like the crocus” (35:1). “Strengthen the weak hands, and make firm the feeble knees… Say to those who have an anxious heart, ‘Be strong; fear not! Behold, your God will come with vengeance… He will come and save you’” (35:3-4). “Then shall the eyes of the blind be opened… then shall the lame man leap like a deer” (35:5-6). Jesus alludes to this passage in Luke 7:22.`,
      [[`Luke 7:20-23`, `Jesus answers John with the deeds of Isaiah 35.`], [`Obadiah 1:1-14`, `Another oracle against Edom.`]],
      [`Chapters 34 and 35 are placed side by side: ruin and bloom. What does the editor want you to feel?`, `“Strengthen the weak hands.” Whose hands do you strengthen?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the pairing?`, `Judgement and salvation are two sides of God’s action.`, `A literary contrast: the desert of Edom and the desert of Israel’s return.`]],
    [36, `A narrative interlude: the siege, a letter and a sick king`,
      `Isaiah 36-39 is borrowed from 2 Kings 18-20 (with some variation) and forms a bridge between the first and second halves of the book. In Isaiah 36-37 the Assyrian field commander taunts Jerusalem, and Hezekiah goes to the temple. Isaiah answers, “Do not be afraid because of the words that you have heard” (37:6). Chapter 38 records Hezekiah’s illness and his psalm: “I said, In the middle of my days I must depart… For Sheol does not thank you; death does not praise you” (38:10, 18). The sign of the shadow and the promise of fifteen years follow. Chapter 39 ends with the Babylonian envoys and Isaiah’s prophecy that all will be carried to Babylon, leading into “Comfort, comfort my people” (40:1).`,
      [[`2 Kings 18:13-20:21`, `The parallel narrative.`], [`Isaiah 40:1-2`, `The next chapter, after the announcement of Babylon.`]],
      [`Isaiah 36-39 stands between two halves of the book. What does that placement suggest?`, `Hezekiah’s psalm says the dead cannot praise. How do you read this against later hopes?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the function of the narrative interlude?`, `It links the Assyrian crisis to the Babylonian one that follows.`, `It shows two types of kings: the faithful Hezekiah and the complacent one.`]],
    [37, `Hezekiah’s prayer, and a 185,000 miracle`,
      `Hezekiah receives Sennacherib’s letter and takes it to the temple: “O LORD of hosts, enthroned above the cherubim, you are the God, you alone, of all the kingdoms of the earth” (37:16). Isaiah sends an answer: “She despises you, she scorns you—the virgin daughter of Zion” (37:22). The sign is agricultural: eat what grows of itself this year, and in the third year sow and reap (37:30). “For out of Jerusalem shall go a remnant… The zeal of the LORD of hosts will do this” (37:32). The angel of the LORD strikes 185,000 in the Assyrian camp, and Sennacherib is killed by his sons in the temple of his god (37:36-38). The Assyrian annals leave out the withdrawal’s cause.`,
      [[`2 Kings 19:14-37`, `The same story.`], [`Psalm 46:1-7`, `A psalm often linked with the deliverance.`]],
      [`Hezekiah spreads a threatening letter before God. What is your equivalent?`, `The sign is a slow agricultural one. Why would a prophet give a sign that takes years?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a short prayer in Hezekiah’s style, spreading out a letter or problem in front of God and naming who God is.`]],
    [38, `The shadow goes backward, and a new beginning in exile`,
      `Isaiah 38-39 moves from Hezekiah’s recovery to the visit of Merodach-baladan’s envoys. Hezekiah’s show of wealth prompts the prophecy of exile: “Behold, the days are coming, when all that is in your house, and that which your fathers have stored up till this day, shall be carried to Babylon” (39:6). Hezekiah’s reply, “The word of the LORD that you have spoken is good. For there will be peace and security in my days” (39:8), is a note of relief or selfishness. Chapter 40 then opens a new section: “Comfort, comfort my people, says your God. Speak tenderly to Jerusalem, and cry to her that her warfare is ended, that her iniquity is pardoned” (40:1-2). Many scholars see chapters 40-55 as addressed to the exiles in Babylon in the sixth century.`,
      [[`2 Kings 20:12-19`, `The same envoy story.`], [`Isaiah 40:1-11`, `The new voice of comfort.`]],
      [`The book moves from a prophecy of exile to a word of comfort for exiles. What does that structure suggest about how Isaiah was read?`, `Why might a book connect an eighth-century prophet to a sixth-century hope?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you explain the shift at chapter 40?`, `A later prophet (“Second Isaiah”) addresses the exiles.`, `The same prophet speaks across time by vision.`]],
    [41, `“Fear not, for I am with you”`,
      `Isaiah 41 opens the second half of the book with a courtroom scene in which God calls the nations to account: “Who stirred up one from the east whom victory meets at every step?” (41:2), probably Cyrus. Then, to Israel: “But you, Israel, my servant, Jacob, whom I have chosen, the offspring of Abraham, my friend… fear not, for I am with you; be not dismayed, for I am your God; I will strengthen you, I will help you, I will uphold you with my righteous right hand” (41:8-10). “Fear not, you worm Jacob, you men of Israel!” (41:14). The “servant” imagery begins here, with Israel as God’s servant, a theme that develops in the four “Servant Songs” (42:1-4; 49:1-6; 50:4-9; 52:13-53:12).`,
      [[`Genesis 15:1`, `“Fear not, Abram, I am your shield.”`], [`Isaiah 42:1-4`, `The first Servant Song.`]],
      [`“You worm Jacob.” What does it mean that God addresses the weak this way?`, `How does a promise to a defeated people differ from a promise to a victorious one?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a one-line “fear not” for someone you know in a hard situation, using the structure “Fear not, for I am with you.”`]],
    [42, `The first Servant Song`,
      `Isaiah 42:1-4 introduces a servant who brings justice gently: “Behold my servant, whom I uphold, my chosen, in whom my soul delights; I have put my Spirit upon him; he will bring forth justice to the nations. He will not cry aloud or lift up his voice… a bruised reed he will not break, and a faintly burning wick he will not quench” (42:1-3). Matthew 12:15-21 quotes the passage of Jesus. The identity of the servant in Isaiah is debated: sometimes it is Israel (41:8; 49:3), sometimes a figure with a mission to Israel (49:5-6). The song says that he “will not grow faint or be discouraged till he has established justice in the earth” (42:4). A gentle, persistent justice is a counterpoint to the conqueror Cyrus.`,
      [[`Matthew 12:15-21`, `Matthew applies the song to Jesus.`], [`Isaiah 49:1-6`, `The second Servant Song, where the servant is also called Israel.`]],
      [`“A bruised reed he will not break.” What does that tell you about the kind of justice he brings?`, `How do you hold the two readings of the servant, as Israel and as an individual?`],
      [`choose`, `Who is the servant?`, `Israel as a people with a mission.`, `An individual who embodies and fulfils the mission, in the later Christian reading.`]],
    [43, `Passing through the waters, and a God who forgets`,
      `Isaiah 43 is among the most consoling chapters: “But now thus says the LORD, he who created you, O Jacob, he who formed you, O Israel: ‘Fear not, for I have redeemed you; I have called you by name, you are mine. When you pass through the waters I will be with you; and through the rivers, they shall not overwhelm you; when you walk through fire you shall not be burned’” (43:1-2). God is a savior who gives Egypt, Cush and Seba as a ransom (43:3). Then he says, “Remember not the former things, nor consider the things of old. Behold, I am doing a new thing; now it springs forth, do you not perceive it?” (43:18-19). And: “I, I am he who blots out your transgressions for my own sake, and I will not remember your sins” (43:25).`,
      [[`Isaiah 51:9-10`, `The exodus recalled as the pattern for a new one.`], [`Revelation 21:5`, `“Behold, I am making all things new.”`]],
      [`The people are told to “remember not the former things” while elsewhere they are told to remember the exodus. How do you hold both?`, `“I will not remember your sins.” What does God’s forgetting mean?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the “new thing”?`, `A new exodus from Babylon.`, `A broader renewal that goes beyond any historical return.`]],
    [44, `A craftsman who cuts a tree and worships it`,
      `Isaiah 44:9-20 is a satire on idol-making. A man cuts down a cedar or cypress; half he burns for warmth and cooking, and “the rest of it he makes into a god, his idol, and falls down to it and worships it… He says, ‘Deliver me, for you are my god!’” (44:17). “He feeds on ashes; a deluded heart has led him astray, and he cannot deliver himself or say, ‘Is there not a lie in my right hand?’” (44:20). Then comes a surprising announcement about Cyrus: “Who says of Cyrus, ‘He is my shepherd, and he shall fulfil all my purpose’; saying of Jerusalem, ‘She shall be built,’ and of the temple, ‘Your foundation shall be laid’” (44:28). Chapter 45:1 calls him “his anointed.”`,
      [[`Psalm 115:4-8`, `A similar mockery.`], [`Ezra 1:1-4`, `Cyrus’s decree.`]],
      [`Where does your own “half of the log” go to warm and the other half to worship?`, `What does it mean that a foreign king is called “shepherd” and “anointed”?`],
      [`rate`, `How alert are you to ordinary things becoming objects of worship?`, `Not alert`, `Very alert`]],
    [47, `Babylon’s fall, and a challenge to the astrologers`,
      `Isaiah 47 is a taunt against “the virgin daughter of Babylon”: “Come down and sit in the dust… sit on the ground without a throne” (47:1). She thought, “I shall be mistress forever” (47:7), and says, “I am, and there is no one besides me” (47:8), the language God uses of himself. Her sorcery will not save her: “Let them stand forth and save you, those who divide the heavens, who gaze at the stars, who at the new moons predict what shall befall you” (47:13). “Behold, they are like stubble; the fire consumes them” (47:14). Chapter 48 then calls the exiles to leave: “Go out from Babylon, flee from Chaldea” (48:20). Revelation 18:4 echoes: “Come out of her, my people.”`,
      [[`Jeremiah 50:1-10`, `Babylon’s fall in Jeremiah.`], [`Revelation 18:4`, `“Come out of her, my people.”`]],
      [`Babylon claims, “I am, and there is no one besides me.” What does it show when a power speaks as God?`, `The call to leave Babylon is both literal and symbolic. What would it mean to leave a “Babylon” today?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read “Come out of Babylon”?`, `As a historical call to return to Judah.`, `As a symbol that Revelation reuses for any oppressive system.`]],
    [49, `“It is too light a thing”`,
      `In the second Servant Song, the servant speaks: “The LORD called me from the womb, from the body of my mother he named my name… He made my mouth like a sharp sword… He said to me, ‘You are my servant, Israel, in whom I will be glorified’” (49:1-3). The servant is discouraged: “I have laboured in vain; I have spent my strength for nothing and vanity” (49:4). God replies, “It is too light a thing that you should be my servant to raise up the tribes of Jacob… I will make you as a light for the nations, that my salvation may reach to the end of the earth” (49:6). Paul and Barnabas quote this verse in Acts 13:47. Zion complains, “The LORD has forsaken me,” and God answers, “Can a woman forget her nursing child?… Yet I will not forget you” (49:14-15).`,
      [[`Acts 13:46-47`, `Paul and Barnabas apply 49:6 to their mission to the Gentiles.`], [`Luke 2:30-32`, `Simeon: “a light for revelation to the Gentiles.”`]],
      [`The servant feels he has laboured in vain, and God answers by enlarging the task. What do you make of that?`, `“Can a woman forget her nursing child?” What does this image add to the picture of God?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write four lines about a time you felt you had laboured in vain, and how a larger purpose might read it differently.`]],
    [51, `Awake, awake, put on strength`,
      `Isaiah 51 calls “you who pursue righteousness, you who seek the LORD: look to the rock from which you were hewn… Look to Abraham your father and to Sarah who bore you” (51:1-2). The prophet then summons God to act: “Awake, awake, put on strength, O arm of the LORD; awake, as in days of old… Was it not you who cut Rahab in pieces, who pierced the dragon? Was it not you who dried up the sea?” (51:9-10). Chapter 52 continues: “Awake, awake, put on your strength, O Zion” (52:1) and “How beautiful upon the mountains are the feet of him who brings good news, who publishes peace” (52:7), which Paul quotes in Romans 10:15. Then the fourth Servant Song begins: “Behold, my servant shall act wisely; he shall be high and lifted up” (52:13).`,
      [[`Romans 10:14-15`, `Paul uses Isaiah 52:7 for preaching.`], [`Isaiah 53:1-12`, `The fourth Servant Song.`]],
      [`The people call God to wake up. What does it mean to say that to God?`, `“Look to the rock from which you were hewn.” What is the rock you came from?`],
      [`predict`, `Before reading Isaiah 53, predict what kind of figure “high and lifted up” will turn out to be.`]],
    [56, `A house of prayer for all peoples`,
      `Isaiah 56 begins a section that opens the community to those excluded by the law of Deuteronomy 23: “Let not the foreigner who has joined himself to the LORD say, ‘The LORD will surely separate me from his people’; and let not the eunuch say, ‘Behold, I am a dry tree’” (56:3). To eunuchs who keep the Sabbath God promises “a monument and a name better than sons and daughters” (56:5). Foreigners who hold fast to the covenant will be brought to “my holy mountain, and make them joyful in my house of prayer… for my house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples” (56:6-7). Jesus quotes this in Mark 11:17 when he clears the temple. The chapter ends with a harsh critique of blind watchmen, “dumb dogs” who love to slumber.`,
      [[`Mark 11:15-17`, `Jesus quotes Isaiah 56:7 in the temple.`], [`Acts 8:26-39`, `The Ethiopian eunuch.`]],
      [`Isaiah overturns a law of exclusion. How do you read a text that revises an earlier one?`, `Jesus quotes “a house of prayer for all peoples” while clearing the temple’s outer court. What does that suggest about the court of the Gentiles?`],
      [`choose`, `What does Isaiah 56 teach about belonging?`, `Faithfulness, not ancestry or body, makes a person part of God’s people.`, `A later prophetic correction of an earlier ritual rule.`]],
    [58, `The fast that God chooses`,
      `Isaiah 58 begins with a complaint: “Why have we fasted, and you see it not?” (58:3). God answers that the people fast while quarrelling and oppressing workers. “Is such the fast that I choose, a day for a person to humble himself? Is it to bow down his head like a reed… Is not this the fast that I choose: to loose the bonds of wickedness, to undo the straps of the yoke, to let the oppressed go free, and to break every yoke? Is it not to share your bread with the hungry and bring the homeless poor into your house; when you see the naked, to cover him?” (58:5-7). “Then shall your light break forth like the dawn… If you pour yourself out for the hungry and satisfy the desire of the afflicted, then shall your light rise in the darkness” (58:8-10). The chapter ends with the Sabbath as a delight.`,
      [[`Matthew 25:35-36`, `The same list: hungry, naked, stranger.`], [`James 1:27`, `“Pure religion… to visit orphans and widows.”`]],
      [`“Is not this the fast that I choose?” What would you give up this week in the way the prophet describes?`, `God’s answer ties worship to economic life. What does that mean for how your community spends and shares?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write your own “fast that God chooses,” three specific acts that you can do in the next month.`]],
    [60, `Arise, shine`,
      `Isaiah 60-62 form a vision of Zion’s restoration, with nations bringing their wealth. “Arise, shine, for your light has come, and the glory of the LORD has risen upon you. For behold, darkness shall cover the earth, and thick darkness the peoples; but the LORD will arise upon you, and his glory will be seen upon you. And nations shall come to your light, and kings to the brightness of your rising” (60:1-3). Matthew’s magi (Matthew 2:1-12) bring gold and frankincense, echoing 60:6. Chapter 61 opens: “The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me, because the LORD has anointed me to bring good news to the poor… to proclaim liberty to the captives… to proclaim the year of the LORD’s favour” (61:1-2), which Jesus reads in the synagogue at Nazareth (Luke 4:16-21). Chapter 62:5 compares God’s joy over Zion to that of a bridegroom.`,
      [[`Luke 4:16-21`, `Jesus reads Isaiah 61 in Nazareth.`], [`Revelation 21:23-26`, `The city needs no sun; nations bring their glory.`]],
      [`Jesus stops reading in the middle of Isaiah 61:2. What do you make of that?`, `The chapter envisions wealth flowing toward Zion. How does that vision compare with Jesus’ idea of good news to the poor?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read Isaiah 60-62?`, `As a vision of the restored Jerusalem after exile.`, `As a prophecy of the kingdom of God that exceeds any historical city.`]],
    [63, `A prayer from the ruins`,
      `Isaiah 63:7 to 64:12 is a communal lament that recalls God’s past acts and asks why he is silent. “In all their affliction he was afflicted, and the angel of his presence saved them; in his love and in his pity he redeemed them” (63:9). The people “rebelled and grieved his Holy Spirit” (63:10). Then the plea: “Look down from heaven and see… Where are your zeal and your might? The stirring of your inner parts and your compassion are held back from me. For you are our Father, though Abraham does not know us, and Israel does not acknowledge us; you, O LORD, are our Father” (63:15-16). “Oh that you would rend the heavens and come down, that the mountains might quake at your presence” (64:1). “We are the clay, and you are our potter; we are all the work of your hand” (64:8).`,
      [[`Mark 1:10`, `At Jesus’ baptism, the heavens are “torn open.”`], [`Jeremiah 18:1-6`, `The potter and the clay.`]],
      [`The people call God Father when their human ancestors do not acknowledge them. What does that say about identity?`, `“Oh that you would rend the heavens.” What would you ask God to break open?`],
      [`rate`, `How comfortable are you praying for God to intervene dramatically?`, `Uncomfortable`, `Comfortable`]],
    [66, `A new heaven and new earth, and a final warning`,
      `Isaiah 65-66 close the book with a promise: “For behold, I create new heavens and a new earth, and the former things shall not be remembered or come into mind” (65:17). “No more shall there be in it an infant who lives but a few days, or an old man who does not fill out his days” (65:20). “The wolf and the lamb shall graze together” (65:25). Chapter 66 starts with a question about the temple: “Heaven is my throne, and the earth is my footstool; what is the house that you would build for me?” (66:1). God looks to “him who is humble and contrite in spirit and trembles at my word” (66:2). The book ends with the nations gathered, and a final image of the fate of rebels: “their worm shall not die, their fire shall not be quenched” (66:24), which Jesus echoes in Mark 9:48.`,
      [[`Revelation 21:1-5`, `John’s vision of the new heaven and new earth.`], [`Mark 9:47-48`, `Jesus quotes the final line.`]],
      [`The book ends on both hope and warning. Why would an editor choose that?`, `“What is the house that you would build for me?” How does this question relate to temples and institutions?`],
      [`choose`, `How should we read “new heavens and a new earth”?`, `As a poetic vision of renewed life in Jerusalem.`, `As a promise of cosmic renewal, which later readers developed.`]]
  ]
};
